# Everything here is consumed on Linux: the guest rootfs, the agent wrappers,
# the entrypoint, the Go sources. Nothing is ever run by a Windows program.
#
# Without this, a checkout on Windows — which is what the WSL2 CI job does, since
# actions/checkout runs Windows-side before the distro sees the tree — applies
# core.autocrlf and rewrites every text file to CRLF. The scripts then carry a
# shebang of `#!/usr/bin/env bash\r`, and executing one fails with
#
#     /usr/bin/env: 'bash\r': No such file or directory
#
# which names the interpreter, not the script, and so reads as though the script
# itself were missing ("fork/exec .../cs-claude-remote-output: no such file or
# directory"). Declaring the whole tree LF makes the checkout correct wherever it
# happens, for anyone working from Windows and not only for CI.
#
# text=auto still lets git detect and leave binaries alone (docs/demo.gif).
* text=auto eol=lf
